  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2025 Angewandte Chemie International Edition article is already showing early scholarly uptake, with 2 citations and 3 documented downstream reuse events. Its influence is concentrated in related nanomedicine and surface-chemistry research, suggesting the paper is being used as a mechanistic reference for layer-by-layer nanoparticle targeting.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article addresses a practical problem in tumor-targeted drug delivery: why anionic polypeptide coatings on layer-by-layer nanoparticles bind selectively to cancer cells. By combining in silico modeling with in vitro experiments, including gene expression analysis and receptor inhibition, the study identifies poly(L-glutamate)-coated nanoparticles as binding with high avidity to SLC1A5, a glutamine transporter overexpressed in cancer-associated contexts. That mechanistic link gives the work a clear translational angle for nanoparticle design.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record is still early, but it is already moving beyond simple acknowledgment. OpenAlex records 2 citations, both in 2026, and the citing literature spans an ACS Nano article and a bioRxiv preprint. Those downstream works focus on nanoparticle surface chemistry, cancer cell retention, immunotherapy, and polymer-surface interactions, which is consistent with the article serving as a conceptual and methodological touchpoint for adjacent research on how nanoparticle coatings control biological binding.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Reuse signals reinforce that pattern. The record shows 3 documented downstream reuse events, including one article, one preprint, and one Europe PMC biomedical article mention. Together, these indicate that the paper is being read not only as a terminal finding but also as a source of ideas and framing for subsequent studies in cancer nanotechnology.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The publication also sits within a well-supported research program: the digest lists funding from the National Institutes of Health, Cancer Research UK, and Howard Hughes Medical Institute, among others, and a GitHub repository is associated with the work. Public discourse and event-data coverage are not available in this record, so the clearest evidence of impact comes from scholarly reuse and early citation activity rather than broader attention.</p>",
